<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kutik-Inshushinak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2100 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kutik-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a> (also read Puzur-Inshushinak) was the last king of the Awan dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, ruling around 2100 BCE. After the collapse of Akkadian power he freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> under his rule and campaigned into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, achieving the first genuine unification of the Elamite lands. He promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-elamite">Linear Elamite</a> script alongside Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, marking a key moment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a>'s foundation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kutik-inshushinak.jpg" alt="Kutik-Inshushinak" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darafsh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Puzur-Inshushinak_with_inscription_in_his_name_(lower_half).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The king's name, written Puzur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a> in Akkadian, means Inshushinak is protection; Kutik-Inshushinak is a possible Elamite reading. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> kinglist he was the last of the Awan dynasty. He gives his father's name as Šimpi-išhuk, an Elamite name, which suggests that he was himself Elamite, and his father, the crown prince, was most likely a brother of Khita. Elam had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> since the time of Sargon. He first served as governor of Susa, perhaps from a young age, and became crown prince when his father died around 2130 BCE. He is the first historical figure in Elamite history.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He first campaigned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>'s behalf, with conquests that went beyond his mission. In the inscription of the Table au Lion he is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ensi">Ensi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and Shakkanakku, military governor, of the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, a title his predecessors Eshpum, Epirmupi and Ili-ishmani had held for Akkad; in another he calls himself Mighty King of Elam. As Akkad weakened under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shar-kali-sharri">Shar-Kali-Sharri</a> he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and united most of Elam. He built extensively on the citadel of Susa, and his inscriptions, at first in Akkadian, came to be written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-elamite">Linear Elamite</a> too: most known Linear Elamite inscriptions date from his reign.</p><p>He took cities of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> and probably Akshak, and so probably weakened Gutian power before the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a>. His reign is now known to have overlapped with that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, who probably retook these northern lands early in his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His achievements did not last. After his death the linear script fell out of use, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a>, who built temples there; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> later came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki dynasty</a>. The lower part of a statue, probably of the king himself, is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> (Sb 55). It lists his victories over neighbouring lands and tells how the king of Shimashki kissed his feet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puzur-Inshushinak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first real unification came around 2100 BCE under <b>Kutik-Inshushinak</b> (Puzur-Inshushinak) of Awan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The last king, <b>Puzur-Inshushinak</b>, built at Susa and left inscriptions in Akkadian and in Linear Elamite, a script largely deciphered only in 2020.</blockquote><a href="/state/awan-dynasty">Awan dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
